}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3a) & =0 \\\\ 3a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507525 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(cx-4), & \\frac{2}{3}<x\\le 1 \\\\ 2x-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nis continuous at $x=1,$ find the value of $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eea34af5-c2e9-54d0-b79f-c71cb047b89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507525 #edb/ref 1074 "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =f(1) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c(1)-4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(c-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(c-4) & =1 \\\\ c-4 & ={2}^{1} \\\\ c & =2+4 \\\\ c & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
